# RKE2 cluster `${cluster_name}`

The cluster is up and its Kubernetes API answered `/readyz`.

| | |
|---|---|
| Kubernetes API | `${api_server_url}` |
| Load balancer | `${lb_dns_name}`%{ if public_load_balancer } (internal: `${internal_lb_dns_name}`)%{ endif } |
| RKE2 version | `${rke2_version}` |
| Nodes | ${server_count} server(s), ${agent_count} agent(s) |
| Hardening profile | `${hardening_profile}` |
| Ingress controller | `${ingress_controller}` |
| Admin certificate valid until | ${admin_cert_expiry} |

## Access

The admin kubeconfig is stored in AWS Secrets Manager (region `${region}`), not in meshStack:

- Secret name: `${kubeconfig_secret_name}`
- Secret ARN: `${kubeconfig_secret_arn}`

Platform team members with access to the AWS account can fetch it with
`scripts/fetch-kubeconfig.sh`.

## Next steps

1. Create a DNS record for your Rancher hostname pointing at `${lb_dns_name}`
   (CNAME, or a Route 53 alias record).
2. Add the **Rancher Manager** building block to this tenant.
